    "lawCode": "Amd §220, Pub Health L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S6086\n\nSPONSOR: BRESLIN\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public health law, in relation to adding a represen-\ntative of freestanding ambulatory surgery centers to the public  health\nand health planning council\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo add a representative of freestanding ambulatory surgery centers to\nthe Public Health and Health Planning Council.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends Public Health Law § 220 to raise the number of\nappointed members of the Public Health and Health Planning Council\n(PHHPC) from 25 to 26, and to require that the additional member be a\nrepresentative of freestanding ambulatory surgery centers.\n\nSection 2 is the effective date: immediate.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nFreestanding ambulatory surgery centers are a growing and increasingly\nimportant part of the health care delivery system in New York State.  As\nof March 2021, there were over 160 FASCs in operation ranging from\nsingle specialty to multi-specialty. FASCs provide high quality, safe,\nconvenient, and cost-efficient surgical care. According to data\npresented to the PHHPC by the NYS Department of Health in May 2019, 87\nFASCs were approved for licensure between 2008 and 2018, while only one\napplication was disapproved.\n\nAccording to the NYSDOH, the percentage of procedures performed in ASCs\nthat were performed in FASCs almost tripled from 2007 to 2017, from\n10.7% to 30.7%. With the ongoing growth of technology that reduces the\ninvasiveness of many surgeries, the types of procedures that are safely\nperformed in ASCs has and will continue to grow.. By one estimate, more\nthan 2,000 different procedures are able to be safely performed at ASCs\nincludingthe most common:cataractsurgeries, colonoscopies, endoscopies\nand orthopedic procedures. In fact, the same NYSDOH data showed that\nbetween 2007 and 2017, the percentage of colonoscopies and biopsies that\nwere performed in ASCs, that were performed in freestanding centers more\nthan doubled, from 20.3% to 46.1%. It is a reasonable expectation that\nthe trend will continue.  The role of ASCs became further evident during\nthe COVID-19 pandemic to assure continued access to non-emergent\nsurgeries, which are important to prevent disease progression and\nexacerbation of symptoms.\n\nWith its important health care planning and policy responsibilities,\nPHHPC needs to be inclusive of all stakeholders in the state's delivery\nsystem. The PHHPC makes decisions and recommendations to the Commission-\ner of Health regarding health care facility establishment, transfer,\nconstruction, and services, and Health Department regulations. It\naddresses policy matters related to health planning, finance and\nreimbursement, public health, and other areas to ensure adequate access\nto high-quality, patient-centered care. The role that freestanding ambu-\n\nlatory surgery centers play in New York State affects all of those poli-\ncy areas, and therefore should have a representative on PHHPC.\n\nPHHPC currently has 25 members appointed by the Governor with the advice\nand consent of the Senate. About half of the appointed members represent\nspecific categories, such as nursing homes, behavioral health, and\ninsurance companies; the other members are chosen from an extremely\nbroad category encompassing practically the entirety of health care\nprovision and payment. Currently, no member of PHHPC is a representative\nof freestanding ambulatory surgery centers, which is an important\nperspective to include as PHHPC deliberates on CONs and regulations\nimpacting the health care delivery system and patients it serves.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
